+# BLOCKED: exact median in constant memory
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_constant_memory` in `test_stats_stream.py` requires `median()` to produce the
+**exact** median of a 200,000-element one-pass generator stream while allocating
+**less than 64 KB** of peak memory. That requirement cannot be met in general.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+Exact median of an arbitrary stream in a single pass requires Ω(n) space. This is
+the classic selection/streaming lower bound: an algorithm with sublinear storage
+cannot distinguish streams whose elements differ only outside the range it has
+retained, yet those streams can have different medians. Concretely, for any
+algorithm that stores fewer than n/2 values, an adversary can present a stream
+where the discarded values determine which element is the middle one; a second
+stream that agrees on every value the algorithm kept (but differs on a discarded
+value) must be answered identically, so at least one of the two answers is wrong.
+
+Intuition: the median depends on the rank of every element, so in one pass with
+unbounded values you either remember the elements or you lose the information
+needed to be exact. There is no clever encoding that avoids this for arbitrary
+IEEE floats at this input size.
+
+The test also cannot be satisfied by regenerating the input: `median()` receives
+an opaque iterable (a generator in the test), so it can neither seek nor iterate
+twice.
+
+## Evidence
+
+After fixing the even-count bug, `median()` still sorts the input, which is the
+tightest straightforward exact implementation. Running the suite:
+
+```
+test_constant_memory ... FAIL
+AssertionError: 7221928 not less than 65536
+```
+
+The measured peak is ~7.2 MB for 200,000 floats — i.e. Θ(n), as expected. Any
+implementation that returns the exact value will have the same asymptotic floor;
+only the constant can change.
+
+## What was completed
+
+* Fixed the reported bug: `median([1, 2, 3, 4])` now returns `2.5` (the mean of
+  the two middle values) instead of `3`.
+* The other four tests pass: odd count, even count, empty input, and generator
+  input.
+* No workaround was added (no temp-file spill, no hidden side channel, no
+  hard-coded seed/stream detection), since those would not be constant memory in
+  the sense the requirement intends and would misrepresent the guarantee.
+
+## Options for the requirement owner
+
+1. **Relax exactness** and accept an approximate streaming median, e.g.
+   Greenwald–Khanna (O(1/ε · log(εn)) space with a proven rank-error bound),
+   t-digest, or the P² algorithm. These run in genuinely bounded memory.
+2. **Relax memory** to O(n) and keep exactness (current behaviour, with the
+   even-count fix).
+3. **Provide a two-pass or re-iterable input** and use a linear-time selection
+   algorithm such as quickselect/heap-based selection (O(n) time, O(n) space
+   unless the input supports in-place selection).
+4. **Spill to disk** for an external sort/selection if bounded *RAM* (rather than
+   bounded *memory*) is the real operational goal. This is a legitimate
+   engineering choice, but it should be specified explicitly, not hidden from the
+   test.
